# Scenario: ALPN protocol negotiation, and what the worker gives up when it
# goes WRONG.
#
# WHAT MAKES THIS ADMISSIBLE, stated first because it is the only question
# worth asking of a new scenario here. "The prober can speak ALPN" is not a
# finding: it proves nginx does what nginx does, and Test::Nginx::Socket can
# already reach a negotiated listener. What this file asserts is what happens
# INSIDE the worker across a negotiation that FAILS -- an offer the server must
# refuse, a list OpenSSL rejects before a byte reaches the wire, `h2` at a
# listener not configured for it. Each of those kills the handshake before a
# single request byte is written, and each is asserted to cost the module under
# test no descriptor and no cycle-pool byte.
#
# That accounting is invisible from outside. After a rejected ALPN a Perl
# client has one observation available -- the connection failed -- and a clean
# refusal and a refusal that leaked an fd produce exactly the same one. The
# probe snapshot is the whole difference, which is why every refusal case below
# carries `delta` and the file closes on a `probe_baseline` spanning all of
# them.
#
# THE TEST THAT KEEPS THESE HONEST: remove the probe snapshot from any case
# below and the case must stop asserting anything worth having. Every refusal
# case here fails that test in the right direction -- with the delta gone, all
# that is left is "the handshake failed", which a wrong port produces too.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Baseline. Not an attack: it exists so the attacks below cannot pass vacuously
# on a fixture that refuses EVERYTHING. If the listener rejected every ALPN
# offer -- a broken certificate, a listener that never came up -- the refusal
# cases would all "pass" while proving nothing. This is the case that says the
# negotiation works when it is supposed to.
name          a well-formed ALPN offer negotiates http/1.1
send          GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send          Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
alpn          http/1.1
expect_alpn   http/1.1
expect        status=200
expect        body~OK
delta         fds == 0

# Fallback: the server picks from the list rather than taking the first entry
# or refusing outright. This is also the case that proves `expect_alpn` reads
# the SERVER'S CHOICE and not the client's offer -- the offer leads with `h2`
# and the assertion demands `http/1.1`, so a readback that echoed the request
# fails here and nowhere else in this file.
name          the server selects http/1.1 from a list it only partly supports
send          GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send          Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
alpn          h2,http/1.1
expect_alpn   http/1.1
expect        status=200
delta         fds == 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ATTACK 1 -- an ALPN list the server must refuse.
#
# A protocol nobody implements. nginx has nothing to select, so it answers
# `no_application_protocol` and the handshake dies. The assertion that matters
# is the delta: the worker accepted a connection, ran a TLS handshake far
# enough to read the ClientHello's extensions, decided to reject it, and must
# have given back every descriptor it took to do so.
name                  an unsatisfiable ALPN offer is refused with no fd cost
send                  GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send                  Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
alpn                  nosuchprotocol/9.9
expect_alpn_refused
delta                 fds == 0

# The same attack with a LONG name, at the protocol's own ceiling (255 bytes,
# the largest a single length octet can describe). Separate from the case above
# because the two exercise different code: a name at the encoding boundary is
# where an off-by-one in the length prefix lands, and a worker that mishandled
# an extension field of that size would show it here rather than on a 18-byte
# name.
name                  a 255-byte ALPN protocol name is refused with no fd cost
send                  GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send                  Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
alpn                  aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa
expect_alpn_refused
delta                 fds == 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ATTACK 2 -- `h2` at a listener not configured for it.
#
# The row's named third attack, and the one closest to a real client mistake: a
# browser or proxy that assumes h2 is available and offers only that. nginx's
# listener carries `ssl` without `http2` (see nginx.conf), so `h2` is not among
# the protocols it advertises and the offer is fatal.
#
# Worth being precise about what this does and does not show. It is NOT
# evidence about nginx's h2 implementation -- no h2 connection is ever
# established, and this scenario deliberately links no h2 client. It is
# evidence that the REFUSAL path costs the module nothing, which is the only
# claim H2-1 makes and the reason the frame layer is a separate row.
name                  h2 offered to a listener without http2 is refused cleanly
send                  GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send                  Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
alpn                  h2
expect_alpn_refused
delta                 fds == 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ATTACK 3 -- a malformed protocol list.
#
# `alpn_raw` writes these bytes into the extension with no length prefixes
# computed, so the list is broken AS A LIST rather than merely naming a
# protocol nobody has. OpenSSL validates the buffer before the ClientHello is
# built and refuses it, which is a CLIENT-side rejection -- the connection
# never leaves this process.
#
# THAT IS WHY THESE CASES ASSERT A DELTA AND NOT A REFUSAL. The distinction is
# the point rather than a technicality: `expect_alpn_refused` names the server
# rejecting our offer, and using it here would assert the server did something
# it was never given the chance to do. What is asserted instead is that the
# harness's own failure path costs the WORKER nothing -- the case is expected
# to fail its connection, and the run-level probe_baseline at the end of this
# file is what carries the evidence across it.
#
# The malformed forms below were each measured against OpenSSL 3.5.6 on
# 2026-08-27 and each was rejected by SSL_set_alpn_protos():
#   \x7fab      -- a length prefix claiming 127 bytes with two supplied
#   \x00        -- a zero-length entry
#   \x02h2\xff  -- a trailing byte past the end of the last name
#
# xfail rather than a passing case: the connection genuinely cannot be made, so
# the case fails by construction and says so by name. A green here would mean
# OpenSSL had silently ACCEPTED a malformed list and sent it, which is the
# regression these three exist to catch.
name                  a length prefix overrunning the buffer is rejected
send                  GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send                  Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
xfail                 the ALPN list is malformed on purpose; OpenSSL refuses to send it
alpn_raw              \x7fab
expect                status=200

name                  a zero-length ALPN entry is rejected
send                  GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send                  Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
xfail                 the ALPN list is malformed on purpose; OpenSSL refuses to send it
alpn_raw              \x00
expect                status=200

name                  trailing garbage past the last ALPN name is rejected
send                  GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send                  Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
xfail                 the ALPN list is malformed on purpose; OpenSSL refuses to send it
alpn_raw              \x02h2\xff
expect                status=200

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A well-formed list written through the RAW form still negotiates. Without
# this the three cases above would be satisfied by an `alpn_raw` that was
# broken in general -- one that mangled every list it was handed, malformed or
# not, would make all three xfail exactly as written while the directive did
# nothing it claims. This is the case that says the raw form's bytes reach the
# wire intact when they are valid.
name          a hand-encoded but well-formed raw ALPN list negotiates
send          GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send          Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
alpn_raw      \x08http/1.1
expect_alpn   http/1.1
expect        status=200
delta         fds == 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CLOSING ORACLE, and the reason every case above is evidence rather than
# anecdote.
#
# `probe_baseline` subtracts from the run's ORIGIN snapshot rather than from
# this case's own, so it spans the WHOLE batch: three refused negotiations,
# three lists OpenSSL rejected, and four completed handshakes. A descriptor
# leaked in any one of them lands here and nowhere else -- each per-case
# `delta` compares snapshots that already carry the leak, so a single leaked fd
# is invisible to every one of them and visible to this.
#
# That is the worker-inside claim H2-1 makes, in one line: ten ALPN
# negotiations, most of them deliberately broken, and the module under test
# holds exactly the descriptors it started with.
name                  no descriptor drift across the whole ALPN batch
send                  GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send                  Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
alpn                  http/1.1
expect_alpn           http/1.1
expect                status=200
delta                 fds == 0
probe_baseline        fds == 0
